# log — structured logging (pure-Zerg stdlib).
#
# Bundled into a program that writes `import "log"`; members are reached through the
# namespace, as `log.info().str("file", path).msg("compiling")`.
#
# THE SHAPE IS A CHAINED BUILDER, and it is right for THIS language rather than merely
# fashionable. Three things Zerg does not have would each have to be waited for by any other
# shape, and the builder steps around all three at once: there are no VARARGS, so one
# key/value per call; there is no `any`, so `str` takes a `str` and `int` takes an `int`; and
# there is no GENERIC STRUCT, so the builder is one concrete type. A `printf`-shaped or a
# map-shaped API needs at least two of the three.
#
# IT IS ALSO THE ANSWER TO LAZY EVALUATION. `log.debug()` at a level that is off hands back a
# DEAD entry: every field method returns immediately, nothing is formatted, and `msg` writes
# nothing. The caller hands over typed values rather than a string it built first, so the
# formatting a disabled line would have done is not skipped — it never happens. That beats a
# closure argument (`log.debug(fn () -> str { … })`), which is noise at every call site and
# allocates an environment per iteration.
#
# WHAT IS STILL PAID is evaluating the ARGUMENTS. `log.debug().str("dump", expensive())` calls
# `expensive()` whether the level is on or not, because Zerg evaluates arguments before the
# call and no API can change that. For a genuinely expensive one the honest answer is the
# explicit guard, and it is why `enabled` is public:
#
#     if log.enabled(log.Level.DEBUG) {
#         log.debug().str("dump", expensive()).msg("state")
#     }
#
# A LOG LINE IS ONE WRITE. This project has already bought that lesson: `zrt_report` split a
# prefix and a message into two writes and a stress test found 830 lines in 24000 pairing one
# kind with another's message. So `msg` builds the whole line, newline included, and hands it
# to a SINGLE `__zrt_write`. Nothing here calls `io.eprintln`, which is two.
#
# THIS MODULE IS ALSO THE TREE'S REFERENCE FOR PROCESS-WIDE MUTABLE STATE. The pattern, and
# the four rules that make it safe to copy, are written above the `unsafe { }` group at the
# foot of this file.
import (
	"io"
	"json"
	"os"
	"time"
)

# --- levels -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# zerolog's set minus the ones Zerg has no use for. `fatal` writes and then exits — it is not
# `panic`, because Zerg already says that with `raise`, and a logger that unwound would be
# competing with the error taxonomy rather than reporting to it.
#
# IT IS AN ENUM AND NOT A SET OF `int` CONSTANTS, and the difference is what the type can
# refuse. Against `pub const INFO := 2`, both `set_level(2)` and `set_level(99)` are legal
# and neither says anything; the enum seals it in both directions — `E340` for an `int`
# where a `Level` belongs and for a `Level` where an `int` does, and `E347` for comparing a
# variant with a number.
#
# THE DEEPER WIN IS THE EXHAUSTIVE `match`. Every renderer below is one, so a level added
# here cannot be added without ranking it, naming it and colouring it: `E428 non-exhaustive
# match: missing variant Level.FATAL` is what a forgotten arm gets. The `int`-constant
# version printed a blank and said nothing.
#
# OFF IS NOT A LEVEL A LINE CAN BE WRITTEN AT. It is the threshold that accepts nothing — it
# is not in the ordering at all, which is what `rank` says by putting it OUTSIDE `0..5`. A
# logger set to it writes nothing; `fatal` still EXITS, because silencing a logger changes
# what is reported, never what is done.
#[derive(Eq)]
pub enum Level {
	TRACE
	DEBUG
	INFO
	WARN
	ERROR
	FATAL
	OFF
}

# rank is the ORDERING, and it is the only thing in this module that knows one. Nothing asks
# `int(Level)` — a variant's discriminant is not consulted anywhere, so the DECLARATION ORDER
# ABOVE IS NOT A CONTRACT: alphabetise those seven names and every program filters exactly as
# it did. Comparing discriminants would make that same edit compile, pass every gate, and
# silently change what every program in the ecosystem writes.
#
# `OFF` IS -1 AND NOT 6, and the reason is what happens when the `< 0` guard in `enabled` is
# lost. Both encodings are correct WITH the guard; they differ in how the loss is noticed,
# and that was measured by deleting it:
#
#   guard deleted            OFF = 6                OFF = -1
#   line at ERROR, lg OFF    still correct          PRINTS — and so does every line
#   line at OFF, lg TRACE    prints                 still correct
#
# With `6` the only thing that breaks is a line written AT `OFF`, which nobody writes, so
# losing the guard is INVISIBLE. With `-1` a logger somebody deliberately silenced starts
# printing everything, which the first person to try it sees. Given two correct encodings,
# take the one whose failure gets noticed.
#
# IT IS MODULE-PRIVATE, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN TIDY. The `-1` encoding is only
# safe while nothing outside can see it: a caller writing `rank(a) > rank(b)` against a
# public `rank` would conclude that `OFF` is the most verbose level of all. The public
# ordering question is `enabled(l) -> bool` — *will this line be written* — which is the
# question a caller actually has.
fn rank(l: Level) -> int {
	return match l {
		Level.TRACE => 0
		Level.DEBUG => 1
		Level.INFO  => 2
		Level.WARN  => 3
		Level.ERROR => 4
		Level.FATAL => 5
		Level.OFF   => -1
	}
}

# parse_level reads a level by NAME — `debug`, not `1`. A number would pin the discriminants
# the rest of this module is careful never to consult, and a name is what a person types into
# an environment variable anyway. Anything else is `nil`, so a caller decides what an
# unrecognised setting means rather than having this module guess.
#
# IT IS `parse_level` AND NOT `parse` for the reason `at_level` is not `at`, and this one was
# measured after the shorter name was written: a `pub` name has no package to be unique
# within, so `pub fn parse` here is `E705` against a module-PRIVATE `fn parse` anywhere in the
# program. A standard library module that exported `parse` would take that word away from
# every program importing it, and `parse` is the commonest private helper name there is. (The
# module already spends `new`, `info`, `warn` and `error` the same way, which is a language
# gap rather than a choice — but it is not a reason to spend one more.)
#
# IT IS A DIVERGE CHAIN AND NOT A `match`, and both halves of that are language rules rather
# than taste: a `match` arm is an expression and may not hold a `return` (`E605`), and every
# arm of one must answer the SAME type (`E322`), so `Level` and `nil` do not unify.
#
# ```zerg
# log.new().level(log.parse_level("warn") ?? log.Level.INFO).enabled(log.Level.ERROR)
# log.new().level(log.parse_level("WARN") ?? log.Level.INFO).enabled(log.Level.DEBUG)
# ```
# ```output
# true
# false
# ```
pub fn parse_level(s: str) -> Level? {
	return Level.TRACE if s == "trace"
	return Level.DEBUG if s == "debug"
	return Level.INFO if s == "info"
	return Level.WARN if s == "warn"
	return Level.ERROR if s == "error"
	return Level.FATAL if s == "fatal"
	return Level.OFF if s == "off"

	return nil
}

# --- the two formats, and where each is chosen ---------------------------------------------
#
# A FORMAT IS AN ENUM FOR THE REASON A LEVEL IS, and it is the cleaner case: a format needs
# no ordering and no ranking, so the enum costs nothing at all. `col` is left a `bool` — a
# `bool` is already a real type with exactly two inhabitants, and enum-ing things for
# symmetry is how a module grows types nobody needed.
#
# PRETTY IS THE DEFAULT, and that departs from zerolog, whose default is JSON. The reason is
# who is on the other end: an unconfigured program is one somebody is running, and a readable
# line is worth more to them than a parseable one. A program whose output is being collected
# says so with `ZERG_LOG=json` or with `log.install(…)`, which is one line in `main` and is a
# decision somebody made rather than one the library guessed.
#
# `ZERG_LOG` PICKS THE FORMAT AND `isatty` DOES NOT, which is the line this module holds and
# the reason `os.isatty` exists at all. Colour is a RENDERING — it is about the device, and
# "is this a device" is exactly what `isatty` answers. A format is a SEMANTIC choice about who
# the output is for, and a program whose logs change SHAPE when they are redirected is a
# program whose logs cannot be read the same way twice. Go's ecosystem draws the line in the
# same place: colour switches itself, `ConsoleWriter` does not.
#
# AN UNRECOGNISED `ZERG_LOG` IS PRETTY rather than an error. A logger that refused to start
# over a misspelt environment variable would be a logger that takes a program down for a
# reason that has nothing to do with it.
#[derive(Eq)]
pub enum Format {
	PRETTY
	JSON
}

# --- where a line goes ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE DESTINATION IS A VALUE CARRYING A MODE, and it was chosen over the two alternatives
# after measuring both. A spec with `#[dyn]` is the shape this would take in a language with
# interfaces, and this compiler has a deferred gap around non-`#[dyn]` provided spec methods;
# a closure would work but a closure that names an imported module is `E735` in this compiler,
# which is every writer that would want to reach `io`. A value needs no dynamic dispatch and
# no capture, so it is the one of the three that is simply available.
#
# THE CHANNEL IS WHAT MAKES A LOGGER TESTABLE, and that is not a side benefit. A logger whose
# only destination is a file descriptor can be asked nothing by a test in the same process —
# there is no reading back a `write(2)`. A `chan[str]` sink hands each finished line to
# whoever holds the other end, so the suite for this module asserts the actual bytes rather
# than "it did not crash".
#
# A CHANNEL SINK MUST HAVE ROOM. A send to a full channel parks the sending coroutine, so a
# test that logs into a channel it will drain later needs the capacity up front — the suite
# uses `chan[str](32)`. A sink whose reader is another coroutine needs no such care.

# Sink is where finished lines go, and THE OPTIONAL IS THE MODE: a channel means send to it,
# and `nil` means standard error. It carried an `int` tag beside the channel for one round and
# that tag was dead — `emit` asked the optional, nothing ever read the tag, and
# `Sink(TO_CHAN, nil)` would have been a stderr sink wearing the wrong label. One field that
# cannot disagree with itself is the whole improvement.
#
# `lines` is `pub` for the reason `testing.Context.events` is: a module-private field must
# carry a default (`E482`), and there is no default channel to write — so the field is public
# and `to_stderr` / `to_chan` are the way one is made.
pub struct Sink {
	pub lines: chan[str]?
}

# to_stderr is the default destination: one `__zrt_write` per line to fd 2.
pub fn to_stderr() -> Sink {
	return Sink(nil)
}

# to_chan sends each finished line, newline included, to `ch`.
pub fn to_chan(ch: chan[str]) -> Sink {
	return Sink(ch)
}

# emit is the ONE place a line leaves this module, so "one write per line" is a property of
# one function rather than a rule every path has to remember.
fn emit(sk: Sink, line: str) {
	if ch := sk.lines {
		ch <- line
		return
	}
	io.ewrite(line)
}

# --- what a logger is ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Logger is one configured logger. It is a VALUE: every method below answers a COPY rather
# than mutating, so a logger handed to a component cannot reconfigure its caller's. That is
# the shape `cli.Command` has and it is the same reason.
#
# A BUILDER DERIVES FROM `this` — `mut c := this; c.f = x; return c` — RATHER THAN LISTING
# EVERY FIELD IN A CONSTRUCTOR. It used to list them, at seven call sites, and the defect that
# shape has is the one no gate can see: add a sixth field and all seven must change, and
# whichever one is missed silently RESETS the new field to its default on that path. There is
# no assertion that could catch it, because every site still compiles and still answers a
# Logger. `mut c := this` is a copy including through the `list` field, so there is nothing
# left to keep in step.
#
# THE FIELDS ARE MODULE-PRIVATE, all of them, and that is the half of it a reader is likelier
# to skip: a caller cannot spell `Logger(…)` positionally, so adding a field breaks nobody
# outside, and a caller cannot write one either, so a logger somebody was handed stays what
# they were handed.
#
# WHAT THE DERIVE COSTS, measured with `make mem-check` rather than guessed: a LIVE line went
# from +18915 to +19110 allocations over 195 rounds — exactly one more per line, because an
# assignment over a field that already holds a live `list` does not release the buffer it
# replaces in this compiler. (Moving the environment to the module-level consts below then took
# it to +18330, so the derive is paid for; the figures are kept because the middle one is what
# the shape itself costs.) The DISABLED line, which is the claim this module is responsible
# for, is +0 throughout. One allocation per written line bought the removal of a defect class
# no gate can see, and the leak itself is a compiler bug that is reported rather than worked
# around here — the same count comes out of `strings.join`, which has nothing to do with
# logging.
#
# THE DEFAULTS ARE THE ENVIRONMENT, AND THEY ARE WRITTEN HERE RATHER THAN IN `new`, which is
# one line that constructs this. A module-private field MUST carry a default (`E482`), so
# `Logger()` exists whatever this module wants; writing the environment into `new` as well
# would have made it a SECOND constructor that silently ignores `ZERG_LOG` — the kind of thing
# nobody finds until a `ZERG_LOG=json` is quietly dropped on the floor.
#
# AND BECAUSE THE THREE CONSTS ARE PRIVATE, `log.Logger()` IS REFUSED OUTSIDE THIS MODULE —
# `E301 ENV_LEVEL is not a public member`, three times, at the construction. So the accidental
# second constructor is not merely made to agree with `new`; it is gone, and `log.new()` is
# the only way a Logger is built from outside. (The message names consts the caller cannot see,
# and calls the module `stdlib` rather than `log`. Both are compiler defects worth reporting;
# neither is a reason to prefer a silent second constructor over a loud refusal.)
pub struct Logger {
	lvl: Level = ENV_LEVEL
	fmt: Format = ENV_FORMAT
	col: bool = ENV_COLOUR
	sink: Sink = to_stderr()
	base: list[json.Field] = []
}

# Entry is one line being built. It exists between `log.info()` and `.msg(…)` and nowhere
# else, which is why `msg` answers nothing: the entry is spent.
#
# `live` IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. A disabled entry carries `live: false`, and every method below
# returns before touching anything — so no field is formatted, no list grows, and NOTHING IS
# ALLOCATED on a line that is not going to be written. `make mem-check` asserts that by
# counting allocations at 5 rounds and at 200 and requiring the two totals to be EQUAL; it is
# the one case in that gate whose claim is "allocated nothing" rather than "leaked nothing".
#
# IT CARRIES THE LOGGER IT CAME FROM, and not a copy of that logger's settings. It held `fmt`,
# `col` and `sink` beside `lvl` for one round, and `at_level` hand-listed all four into the
# constructor — which is the SAME defect the builders above stopped having, one level down and
# on the path every written line takes. A sixth rendering setting would have had to be threaded
# through the Logger field, the Entry field, that positional list and `render`, and the site
# that dropped it would have silently rendered the Entry default on every line. Now the
# constructor takes `this`, and there is nothing to keep in step.
#
# It costs nothing: a `Logger` is a value whose only heap field is the copy-on-write `base`
# this entry was already sharing, so carrying it is a refcount and not a copy. `make mem-check`
# still puts the disabled line at +0.
#
# `log.Entry()` IS REACHABLE FROM OUTSIDE and is INERT, which is why it is left alone. Every
# field must carry a default (`E482` again), so a zero-argument construction exists whatever
# this module wants; the two defaults that matter make it harmless — `live` is false, so every
# method returns at once and nothing is written, and `lvl` is `OFF`, so `msg` does not exit
# either. `Logger` closes the same hole a different way, by giving its defaults private consts
# to name; here there is nothing to close.
pub struct Entry {
	live: bool = false
	lvl: Level = Level.OFF
	lg: Logger = Logger()
}

# new answers a logger configured from the environment, writing to standard error. It is the
# INSTANCE constructor, and the global further down is one of these rather than a second
# implementation beside it.
#
# IT IS ONE LINE ONTO THE ZERO VALUE, which is what makes it the ONLY constructor rather than
# the first of two: `Logger()` carries the same defaults because they are the same defaults,
# and outside this module it is refused outright (see the struct above). `new` exists to be
# the NAME a caller and this file's own documentation use.
#
# ```zerg
# log.new().level(log.Level.WARN).enabled(log.Level.INFO)
# log.new().level(log.Level.WARN).enabled(log.Level.ERROR)
# ```
# ```output
# false
# true
# ```
pub fn new() -> Logger {
	return Logger()
}

# --- the environment, read once ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE THREE `#[allow(L102)]` BELOW ARE SUPPRESSING A FALSE POSITIVE, and that is worth stating
# once rather than three times. Each of these functions IS called — by a module-level `const`
# further down, which is what `Logger`'s field defaults name. `L102`'s reachability walk does
# not visit a top-level `const` initialiser (nor a field default; both spellings were tried),
# so it reports all three as dead. The linter is wrong and the code is right; the defect is
# reported rather than worked around by writing the environment logic inline, which would trade
# three named, documented functions for three unreadable const expressions.

# env_level is `ZERG_LOG_LEVEL`, by name, and anything it does not recognise is `INFO`. It is
# the one way a level is chosen without editing the program, which matters more here than it
# would elsewhere: the only other way is `log.install(…)` in `main`, and the person who wants
# a debug line is often not the person who can rebuild.
#[allow(L102)]
fn env_level() -> Level {
	return parse_level(os.env("ZERG_LOG_LEVEL") ?? "") ?? Level.INFO
}

# env_format is `ZERG_LOG`, and anything it does not recognise is `pretty`.
#
# ALL THREE READERS ARE WRITTEN WITH `??` RATHER THAN `if s := v { … }`, and that is not a
# style choice. `zerg fmt` collapses a lone `return` inside an `if` into the postfix form, and
# `return false if s := off` DOES NOT PARSE — the trailing `if` on a diverge takes a bare
# CONDITION and a binding head is not one (`E205`). The formatter turns a valid program into an
# invalid one, and `fmt --check` then calls the invalid one canonical, which is how this module
# broke itself once. Reported; four lines reproduce it. `??` is the shorter answer anyway.
#[allow(L102)]
fn env_format() -> Format {
	v := os.env("ZERG_LOG") ?? ""
	return Format.JSON if v == "json"
	return Format.PRETTY
}

# env_colour is the two questions a rendering decision is made of, in the order the convention
# gives them: `NO_COLOR` is an override and wins, and otherwise colour follows the DEVICE —
# `os.isatty(2)`, since standard error is where a line goes.
#
# `NO_COLOR` IS ABOUT ITS PRESENCE (https://no-color.org), so any value at all turns colour
# off — including the empty string, because a variable somebody exported empty is still a
# variable somebody exported. That is why the sentinel below is a string nobody sets rather
# than `""`: coalescing to `""` would make an exported-empty `NO_COLOR` indistinguishable from
# an unset one, which is the one distinction this variable is made of.
#[allow(L102)]
fn env_colour() -> bool {
	off := os.env("NO_COLOR") ?? NO_COLOR_UNSET
	return false if off != NO_COLOR_UNSET
	return os.isatty(2)
}

# NO_COLOR_UNSET is the value `env_colour` uses to mean "the variable is not there". It is not
# a value anybody exports — it opens with U+0001, which is the whole requirement. (U+0000 is
# not available:  refuses a NUL in a string literal.)
const NO_COLOR_UNSET := "\u{1}NO_COLOR is not set"

# The three answers, read ONCE. A module-level `const` may call a function, and these are the
# defaults `Logger`'s fields carry — so the environment is asked at module init rather than at
# every construction. That is not a micro-optimisation: `env_colour` calls `os.isatty(2)`,
# which is a SYSCALL, and `testing.zg` builds a fresh logger for every note a test writes.
#
# They are declared AFTER `NO_COLOR_UNSET` because a top-level `const` is evaluated in source
# order, and `env_colour` reads that sentinel.
const ENV_LEVEL := env_level()
const ENV_FORMAT := env_format()
const ENV_COLOUR := env_colour()

impl Logger {
	# level answers a COPY of this logger writing at `l` and above.
	#
	# ```zerg
	# log.new().level(log.Level.OFF).enabled(log.Level.FATAL)
	# log.new().level(log.Level.TRACE).enabled(log.Level.TRACE)
	# ```
	# ```output
	# false
	# true
	# ```
	pub fn level(l: Level) -> This {
		mut c := this
		c.lvl = l
		return c
	}

	# to answers a COPY writing to `sk`.
	pub fn to(sk: Sink) -> This {
		mut c := this
		c.sink = sk
		return c
	}

	# format answers a COPY writing `Format.PRETTY` or `Format.JSON`. It is what `ZERG_LOG`
	# sets, and it is a method as well as an environment variable because a program that knows
	# where its output goes should not have to be told by its own launcher.
	pub fn format(f: Format) -> This {
		mut c := this
		c.fmt = f
		return c
	}

	# colour answers a COPY with ANSI colour on or off. The default is the environment's —
	# `NO_COLOR` first, then whether standard error is a terminal — and this is the override
	# for a program that knows better, which is mostly a test.
	pub fn colour(on: bool) -> This {
		mut c := this
		c.col = on
		return c
	}

	# enabled reports whether a line at `l` would be written. It is PUBLIC because the
	# builder's one residual cost — evaluating the arguments — has no API answer, so a caller
	# holding a genuinely expensive value needs a way to ask before it computes one.
	#
	# THE `< 0` GUARD IS WHAT MAKES `OFF` SILENT, including for a line written AT `OFF`, which
	# is the one case a threshold comparison alone gets wrong. See `rank`.
	pub fn enabled(l: Level) -> bool {
		floor := rank(this.lvl)
		return false if floor < 0
		return rank(l) >= floor
	}

	# with_str answers a copy carrying `k=v` on every line it writes from here on. It is what
	# makes an instance logger worth holding: a component names itself once instead of
	# repeating it at every call site.
	#
	# ```zerg
	# log.new().with_str("component", "lexer").with_int("worker", 3).enabled(log.Level.INFO)
	# ```
	# ```output
	# true
	# ```
	pub fn with_str(k: str, v: str) -> This {
		mut c := this
		json.put_str(c.base, k, v)
		return c
	}

	# with_int is with_str for a number.
	pub fn with_int(k: str, v: int) -> This {
		mut c := this
		json.put_int(c.base, k, v)
		return c
	}

	# --- one method per level, and the one the language would not let be a method ---
	#
	# `at_level` IS PUBLIC AND `debug()` IS NOT WRITTEN, and that is a language rule rather
	# than a preference. `debug` is a RESERVED METHOD NAME in Zerg: `display` and `debug` are
	# the two renderings every value has (docs/runtime/format.md), so a method by either name
	# must answer the `str` the value shows as — and `E361 Logger.debug() answers the str the
	# value shows as, and this one does not` is what a level method by that name gets.
	#
	# The rule is about METHODS ONLY, measured: the free `log.debug()` further down is the
	# level's own name and is accepted, so the GLOBAL surface has all six. On an instance the
	# sixth is `lg.at_level(log.Level.DEBUG)`, which invents no name for a level that already
	# has one — the alternative was a `dbg` that exists only because of a compiler rule and can
	# drift from the constant it stands for.
	#
	# AND IT IS `at_level` RATHER THAN `at` because of the OTHER rule: the global surface
	# below wants the same function as a free one, and a free `pub at` is `E705` against the
	# compiler's own lexer, which has a module-private `at`. A `pub` name has no package to
	# be unique within, so a private name in one module can take a public one away from
	# another. One name for one concept was worth four characters.

	# at_level begins a line at any level. It is the general form the five below are written
	# on, and it is what an instance writes for DEBUG.
	pub fn at_level(l: Level) -> Entry {
		# THE ENTRY BORROWS THE LOGGER'S FIELD LIST rather than being handed a fresh one, and
		# that is what makes the disabled path free: `[]` is an allocation, and a `list` is
		# copy-on-write, so sharing costs a refcount and the first `.str()` pays for the copy
		# it actually needs. A dead entry never reads `fs` at all.
		return Entry(this.enabled(l), l, this)
	}

	pub fn trace() -> Entry {
		return this.at_level(Level.TRACE)
	}

	pub fn info() -> Entry {
		return this.at_level(Level.INFO)
	}

	pub fn warn() -> Entry {
		return this.at_level(Level.WARN)
	}

	pub fn error() -> Entry {
		return this.at_level(Level.ERROR)
	}

	# fatal writes the line and then exits with status 1.
	pub fn fatal() -> Entry {
		return this.at_level(Level.FATAL)
	}
}

impl Entry {
	# str adds `k=v` to this line. On a dead entry it is a return and nothing else.
	pub fn str(k: str, v: str) -> This {
		return this if not this.live
		mut c := this
		json.put_str(c.lg.base, k, v)
		return c
	}

	# int adds a number.
	pub fn int(k: str, v: int) -> This {
		return this if not this.live
		mut c := this
		json.put_int(c.lg.base, k, v)
		return c
	}

	# bool adds a flag.
	pub fn bool(k: str, v: bool) -> This {
		return this if not this.live
		mut c := this
		json.put_bool(c.lg.base, k, v)
		return c
	}

	# dur adds an elapsed nanosecond count, rendered the way a person reads one. It is a
	# method rather than `str(k, time.duration(ns))` written at each call site so that an
	# elapsed time is never logged as a bare nanosecond integer, which is the shape nobody can
	# compare at a glance.
	pub fn dur(k: str, ns: int) -> This {
		return this if not this.live
		return this.str(k, time.duration(ns))
	}

	# err adds what an `Err` said, under the key `error` — a method rather than a convention
	# so that every program in this ecosystem puts a failure under the same key, which is most
	# of what structured logging is for.
	pub fn err(e: Err) -> This {
		return this if not this.live
		return this.str("error", f"{e}")
	}

	# msg finishes the line and writes it. It is the TERMINAL: an Entry has no other exit, so
	# a `log.debug()` nobody finishes writes nothing rather than half a line.
	pub fn msg(text: str) {
		if this.live {
			line := render(time.now(), this.lvl, this.lg, text)
			emit(this.lg.sink, line)
		}

		# THE EXIT IS NOT INSIDE THAT `if`. A `fatal` at a silenced level still ends the
		# program: quietening a logger changes what is REPORTED, never what is DONE.
		if this.lvl == Level.FATAL {
			os.exit(1)
		}
	}
}

# --- rendering ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# render builds the whole line INCLUDING its newline, which is what makes `emit` one write.
# It is a pure function of its arguments, so the suite asks it the question through a channel
# sink rather than by capturing a stream.
fn render(now: int, lvl: Level, lg: Logger, text: str) -> str {
	stamp := time.rfc3339(now)
	return as_json(stamp, lvl, text, lg.base) if lg.fmt == Format.JSON
	return as_pretty(stamp, lvl, lg.col, text, lg.base)
}

# as_pretty is the human line: a timestamp, a three-letter level, the message, then the
# fields. Three letters and not the whole word so that every line is the same width here and
# the message begins in the same column.
fn as_pretty(stamp: str, lvl: Level, col: bool, text: str, fs: list[json.Field]) -> str {
	mut out := f"{stamp} {tag(lvl, col)} {text}"

	# TWO SPACES BEFORE THE FIRST FIELD and one between the rest, so the message and the
	# structured part are told apart at a glance — a message is prose and may contain a
	# single space, and without the wider gap the eye has to parse for the first `=`.
	mut sep := "  "
	for f in fs {
		v := shown(f.val)
		out = out + sep + f.key + "=" + v
		sep = " "
	}
	return out + "\n"
}

# as_json is the machine line. It is built through `json` and not by hand, which is the whole
# reason that module was promoted out of the language server: a message carrying a quote, a
# newline or a tab is the ordinary case for a compiler's diagnostics, and one escaper in the
# tree is one place for that to be right.
#
# THE KEYS ARE SHORT AND FIXED — `t`, `l`, `msg` — and they come first, in that order, because
# the field order is the order they were put and a transcript that is read by eye wants the
# same three at the front of every line.
fn as_json(stamp: str, lvl: Level, text: str, fs: list[json.Field]) -> str {
	mut out: list[json.Field] = []
	json.put_str(out, "t", stamp)
	json.put_str(out, "l", level_name(lvl))
	json.put_str(out, "msg", text)
	for f in fs {
		json.put(out, f.key, f.val)
	}

	return json.encode(json.of_obj(out)) + "\n"
}

# tag is the three-letter level a pretty line is prefixed with, coloured when the destination
# is a terminal and `NO_COLOR` is unset.
#
# ONLY THE LEVEL IS COLOURED. It is the field a reader scans for, and colouring the message
# or the values would fight with whatever they contain — a diagnostic that already carries
# escape codes, or a value that is going to be copied out of the terminal and pasted.
fn tag(lvl: Level, col: bool) -> str {
	name := short_name(lvl)
	return name if not col
	return colour_of(lvl) + name + "\u{1b}[0m"
}

# short_name, level_name and colour_of are the three renderings of a level, and each is an
# EXHAUSTIVE `match` rather than a chain of comparisons with a fallback. That is the whole
# reason `Level` is an enum: a level added above cannot reach a program without an answer
# here, because `E428` names the variant each of these three forgot. The chain they replaced
# ended in `"???"` and would have shipped one silently.
#
# THE `OFF` ARM IS UNREACHABLE AND SAYS `"OFF"` ANYWAY. `msg` never renders one — `enabled`
# is false for every level once the threshold is `OFF`. A blank would look like a formatting
# bug if it ever did leak, and a name that is honest costs three characters.
fn short_name(lvl: Level) -> str {
	return match lvl {
		Level.TRACE => "TRC"
		Level.DEBUG => "DBG"
		Level.INFO  => "INF"
		Level.WARN  => "WRN"
		Level.ERROR => "ERR"
		Level.FATAL => "FTL"
		Level.OFF   => "OFF"
	}
}

# level_name is the level as a JSON line spells it — the whole word, lower case, which is what
# every log shipper this would be read by already expects. It is also what `parse_level` reads,
# so the two are inverses and a round trip through an environment variable is exact.
fn level_name(lvl: Level) -> str {
	return match lvl {
		Level.TRACE => "trace"
		Level.DEBUG => "debug"
		Level.INFO  => "info"
		Level.WARN  => "warn"
		Level.ERROR => "error"
		Level.FATAL => "fatal"
		Level.OFF   => "off"
	}
}

# colour_of is the SGR sequence a level opens with. They are the eight-colour codes rather
# than 256-colour or truecolour, because those are what every terminal since the 1980s has and
# a logger is the last place to want a capability check.
fn colour_of(lvl: Level) -> str {
	return match lvl {
		Level.TRACE => "\u{1b}[90m"
		Level.DEBUG => "\u{1b}[36m"
		Level.INFO  => "\u{1b}[32m"
		Level.WARN  => "\u{1b}[33m"
		Level.ERROR => "\u{1b}[31m"
		Level.FATAL => "\u{1b}[91m"
		Level.OFF   => "\u{1b}[0m"
	}
}

# shown renders one field's value for a person. A string that would be AMBIGUOUS unquoted —
# empty, or carrying a space, a quote, a backslash, an `=` or a control character — goes
# through `json.encode`, which is the tree's ONE escaper. Everything else is written bare,
# because `line=12` and `file=a.zg` are what somebody actually reads.
#
# THE TEST FOR "IS THIS A STRING" IS `as_str(v) != ""`, and that reads like a trick until the
# cases are laid out. `json`'s accessors are total and a caller outside it cannot match on the
# variants, so this is the only question available — and it happens to partition exactly right:
# a number, a bool and a `null` all answer "" and all want `encode`, which writes them bare;
# an EMPTY string also answers "" and wants `encode` too, because a bare `k=` is not a value.
# Every case that reaches the last line is a non-empty string with nothing ambiguous in it.
fn shown(v: json.Val) -> str {
	s := json.as_str(v)
	return s if s != "" and not needs_quotes(s)
	return json.encode(v)
}

fn needs_quotes(s: str) -> bool {
	for c in bytearray(s) {
		return true if int(c) <= 32 or c == b'"' or c == b'\\' or c == b'='
	}
	return false
}

# --- the global logger, and the pattern to copy for any other one ------------------------------
#
# THE PACKAGE PROVIDES TWO SURFACES AND NEITHER REPLACES THE OTHER: a logger you hold and pass
# (`log.new()`, above) and a system-wide one that works with no plumbing (`log.info()`, below).
# That is zerolog's shape, and the reason is that a component with its own level or its own
# fields wants the first while everything else wants the second.
#
# ================================================================================================
# THE PATTERN — process-wide mutable state in Zerg.
#
# This is the tree's reference for a global that has to be REPLACEABLE once the program is
# running. Everything needed to copy it correctly is in this comment; nothing else in this
# module has to be read. It is the only module-level `unsafe { mut … }` in the shipping tree,
# and the normative rule it is an exception to is in docs/runtime/package.md.
#
#     unsafe {
#         mut log_cell := new()                # 1. one cell, private, named, already valid
#     }
#
#     pub fn install(lg: Logger) {             # 2. one writer, taking a value already built
#         log_cell = lg
#     }
#
#     pub fn info() -> Entry {                 # 3. every reader, one line onto the instance
#         return log_cell.info()
#     }
#
# 1. ONE CELL, MODULE-PRIVATE, ALREADY VALID — AND NAMED AFTER THE MODULE.
#
#    A bare `mut` at module level is refused: `E358 the top-level binding `level` may not be
#    `mut` outside a module-level `unsafe { … }` group — module state is immutable, and shared
#    mutable state travels by value or through channels`. Inside the group it is allowed, and
#    `pub` on it is refused in turn: `E484 the mutable global `log_cell` may not be `pub` — a
#    module-level `unsafe { … }` group is one module's bargain with its own author`. So
#    "configure by function" is not the RECOMMENDED way here, it is the ONLY way the language
#    permits, and nothing is left for a reviewer to remember.
#
#    IT IS INITIALISED AT ITS DECLARATION, by the same PUBLIC constructor a caller uses. There
#    is then no before-install state and no "has anyone configured this yet" to get wrong: a
#    program that never calls `install` logs at the default, reading the cell is total on the
#    first line of `main`, and a test restores the default by REBUILDING it — which is why no
#    `current()` is needed to have a suite.
#
#    THE INITIALISER MUST BE TOTAL, and this is the constraint the sentence above is really
#    making. It runs at module init, before `main`, where there is no `guard` and no place to
#    report from: a cell whose constructor RAISES kills the program with a bare
#    `IOError: cannot open file` and exit 1, before the line in `main` that would have
#    installed a good value can run — measured. If your constructor can fail, initialise the
#    cell with the default and do the fallible construction in `main`, under a `guard`, and
#    `install` only what came back.
#
#    AND THE ORDER OF WHAT IT READS IS A SILENT TRAP. A top-level `const` may call a function,
#    and a `const` reached THROUGH such a call is not yet assigned if it is declared later:
#    `const A := mk()` above `const B := "x"`, with `mk()` reading `B`, gives the ZERO value
#    with no diagnostic at all — an empty string in the probe that measured it, and a SEGV
#    (exit 139) when the same shape is moved inside this module. A compiler defect worth
#    reporting. What defends this module is not the comment: `NO_COLOR_UNSET` is declared above
#    the `ENV_*` consts that read it, and `scripts/log-check.sh` fails loudly if that is ever
#    swapped — measured by swapping it.
#
#    NAME THE CELL AFTER THE MODULE — `log_cell`, `settings_cell` — and not `process` or
#    `state`. This compiler flattens every module into one namespace, so two modules that copy
#    this pattern and both name their cell `process` cannot be linked, PRIVATE OR NOT:
#    `E706 … both define `process` — this compiler flattens every module into one namespace`.
#    Measured with a second module built on this shape.
#
# 2. ONE WRITER, AND IT TAKES A VALUE THAT IS ALREADY BUILT.
#
#    This replaced a `set_level` / `set_format` / `set_colour` / `set_sink` family, and the
#    replacement is a DELETION rather than a rename: the module already had four pure builders,
#    so the setters were a second way to say the same thing that happened to mutate shared
#    state four times instead of once. `install(new().level(…).format(…))` assembles the value
#    off to one side and stores it once — four windows for a reader to see a half-configured
#    logger become one. The rule that keeps it one writer is not visible in the source, so
#    `scripts/log-check.sh` reads this file and asserts it: every mention of the cell is the
#    declaration, that one assignment, or a `return log_cell.…` delegation.
#
#    THE NAME IS `install` AND NOT `level`, because `level` already means *derive a logger at
#    this level*, and one word may not be pure on an instance and effectful on the module.
#    (`enabled` lives in both places precisely because it means the same thing in both.)
#    Note that the writer's name is program-global too, by `E705` rather than `E706`: a second
#    module cannot also call its writer `install`, so this one is a word `log` is spending.
#
#    THERE IS NO `current()`. A `current().level(…)` interface would read as mid-flight
#    reconfiguration, and rule 4 is why the cell is not safe for that. Not offering a way to
#    start from the current value is what makes configuring read as the startup act it is.
#
# 3. THE GLOBAL IS AN INSTANCE, NEVER A SECOND CODE PATH.
#
#    Every public function below is one line that reaches the cell and calls a method that
#    already exists — `log_cell.info()`, not `log_cell.at_level(Level.INFO)`, so the level a
#    name means is written once. There is exactly one level check, one builder and one writer
#    in this module. A global that grew its own comparison would be a second logger drifting
#    from the first, which is the argument that keeps one JSON escaper in this tree.
#
#    THE RULE ASSUMES THE GLOBAL IS AN INSTANCE OF A TYPE THAT ALREADY HAS THE API. For a
#    global that is a bare `int` or a `map`, there is no instance to delegate to and rule 3
#    says nothing; and for one whose whole point is being MUTATED as the program runs — a
#    counter, a registry — this pattern does not apply at all. That case wants one owning
#    coroutine and a channel, which is what `E358`'s own message points at.
#
# 4. WHAT IT COSTS, WITHOUT ROUNDING IT UP.
#
#    A `Logger` holds a `list` and a `Sink`, so `log_cell = lg` is SEVERAL machine stores and
#    not one. GUARANTEED: nothing whatever about a read that runs while `install` runs — a
#    reader can see this level with that sink, or a field list mid-swap. This project has found
#    four real races with `ZRT_SEED` and `REPS 150`, so that is measured behaviour and not a
#    theoretical caveat.
#
#    THE RULE, WHICH IS A RULE AND NOT A GUARANTEE: CONFIGURE AT STARTUP, FROM ONE COROUTINE,
#    THEN READ. Under it every read happens after the only write and there is no race left to
#    have. `log.new()` is the answer for anything that must change while the program runs — an
#    instance is a value, and a value handed to a component races with nobody.
#
#    NO GATE HOLDS THIS ONE, and saying so is part of stating it. A stress case was considered
#    and is not shipped: coroutines here are cooperative and there is no parking point inside
#    `install`, so many-readers-one-installer comes back clean whether the property holds or
#    not — the same reason scripts/log-check.sh gives for asserting "one write per line" over
#    the source instead. A gate that cannot fail is worse than an absent one.
#
# INSTALLING TWICE IS LEGAL AND THE LAST ONE WINS, silently. It is the pattern's one silent
# failure mode: two `install` calls in two startup helpers leave the second, and nothing
# anywhere says the first happened. Refusing a second install was considered and rejected — a
# test suite installs per test, and a rule that only `main` may configure is not one the
# language can check.
#
# WHEN NOT TO COPY THIS AT ALL. A module-level `const` may CALL A FUNCTION — `const P := mk()`
# compiles — so a global that never changes after startup needs no `unsafe`, and should not
# have one. This shape is only for a value that must be REPLACED once the program is running,
# and `log` is that case only because a level has to be settable from `main` in a program whose
# functions were handed no logger.
#
# WHAT WOULD TAKE THE `unsafe` AWAY, and neither is a rewrite of this module:
#
#   - an immutable binding holding a stdlib `Atomic[T]`, which is the safe alternative GRAMMAR
#     names for exactly this. `atomic` today is `Atomic[int]` and cannot be imported at all
#     (`E511`: a generic struct is a form this compiler has not built), so this waits on
#     generic structs and on `Atomic[T]` reaching a struct payload.
#   - a language in which a whole struct store is one atomic operation, which would make
#     rule 4's window close rather than merely be avoided.
# ================================================================================================
unsafe {
	mut log_cell := new()
}

# install replaces the global logger, and it is the only thing in this module that writes the
# cell. Call it in `main`, before anything else runs; rule 4 above is why "before".
pub fn install(lg: Logger) {
	log_cell = lg
}

# enabled is the global's level check, and it is public for the reason the method is: a caller
# holding a value that costs something to compute needs to ask before it computes one.
pub fn enabled(lvl: Level) -> bool {
	return log_cell.enabled(lvl)
}

# at_level begins a line on the global logger at any level.
pub fn at_level(lvl: Level) -> Entry {
	return log_cell.at_level(lvl)
}

pub fn trace() -> Entry {
	return log_cell.trace()
}

# debug is a FREE function and can therefore carry the level's own name — `debug` is reserved
# only as a METHOD (see `Logger.at_level` above). It is also the one global level function with
# no method to delegate to, which is why it is the only one that names its level here.
pub fn debug() -> Entry {
	return log_cell.at_level(Level.DEBUG)
}

pub fn info() -> Entry {
	return log_cell.info()
}

pub fn warn() -> Entry {
	return log_cell.warn()
}

pub fn error() -> Entry {
	return log_cell.error()
}

# fatal writes the line and exits 1, on the global logger.
pub fn fatal() -> Entry {
	return log_cell.fatal()
}
